---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import EvaluationFigure from '../components/figures/EvaluationFigure.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Evaluation & Reporting"
  eyebrow="Project Evaluation and Reporting"
  section="(f)"
  lede="Two purposes: ongoing feedback that improves the program while it runs, and evidence of whether the Site achieved its objectives — collected through the CRA CERP REU Evaluation Surveys and an external evaluator."
  description="The formative and summative evaluation plan for the ASPIRE REU Site, including instruments and assessment schedule."
>
  <div class="wrap section">
    <p class="narrow">
      The team collects quantitative and qualitative data through surveys, activity logs, mentor–student
      meeting notes, and student research products. Evaluation uses the <b>CRA CERP REU Evaluation
      Surveys</b> to assess students' growth, strengths, computing experiences, and professional
      development, while enabling comparison with similar REU programs. After providing informed consent,
      students complete a pre-program survey, a mid-program review, and a post-summer survey. Findings are
      reviewed after each summer to strengthen activities for subsequent cohorts and are summarized in
      annual and final reports to NSF.
    </p>

    <Figure
      title="Evaluation logic model and assessment schedule."
      caption="Formative points feed back into the running program; summative points measure whether the Site met its objectives. Follow-up continues for at least five years after participation."
    >
      <EvaluationFigure />
    </Figure>

    <h2 id="implementation">I. Evaluation of project activities and implementation</h2>
    <p class="narrow">This strand examines how well program activities are implemented and identifies opportunities for continuous improvement.</p>
    <div class="grid grid-3">
      <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin-top:0">Activities and implementation</h4>
        <p style="font-size:.92rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0">
          An activity log tracks recruitment, orientation, training, seminars, mentoring, presentations,
          research outputs, and student progress. Projects are organized into weekly milestones beginning
          with tutorials, reproducible examples, and validation tasks and progressing toward open-ended
          research, each with a clear research question, goals, and accessible research materials. Weekly
          meetings and mid-summer feedback keep project scope, training, and research activities aligned
          with students' progress, interests, and strengths.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin-top:0">Engagement and research progress</h4>
        <p style="font-size:.92rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0">
          The team documents participation, engagement, milestone completion, and research outputs. Mentors
          use this to provide individualized learning opportunities — tutorials, additional mentoring, peer
          collaboration, or more advanced research tasks — based on each student's progress and interests.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin-top:0">Recruitment and selection</h4>
        <p style="font-size:.92rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0">
          The team tracks applications, interviews, applicants' institutions and academic backgrounds,
          participants recruited from outside TTU and from institutions with limited research
          opportunities, and characteristics of the applicant pool. Recruitment data are reviewed annually
          to strengthen outreach, application review, and student–project matching.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="outcomes">II. Evaluation of impact on student learning outcomes</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-3">
      <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin-top:0">Knowledge, skills, and confidence</h4>
        <p style="font-size:.92rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0">
          Pre- and post-program surveys, meeting notes, and research products (posters, presentations,
          manuscripts) assess growth in AI and HPC research: formulating research questions, working with
          AI models and HPC technologies, conducting experiments, analyzing results, and communicating
          findings.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin-top:0">Professional growth and future plans</h4>
        <p style="font-size:.92rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0">
          CRA CERP evaluations assess students' confidence, interest in graduate education, awareness of
          computing research and career pathways, and future educational and career plans.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4 style="margin-top:0">Long-term outcomes</h4>
        <p style="font-size:.92rem;color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0">
          The team maintains contact through email, LinkedIn, and Discord for at least five years.
          Follow-up data track continued research engagement, research outputs, graduate education, and
          career pathways.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="cerp">III. External evaluation with CRA CERP</h2>
    <p class="narrow">
      To measure project success qualitatively and quantitatively, the Site works with the Computing
      Research Association's <b>Center for Evaluating the Research Pipeline (CERP)</b>, which conducts
      evaluations of NSF CISE REUs and interfaces with the Texas Tech Institutional Review Board, which
      oversees the University's Human Research Protection Program. CERP runs longitudinal quantitative
      surveys across three time points: a pre-test at the beginning of the REU, a post-test at the end,
      and a follow-up one year later.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>What CERP measures</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Self-perceptions</b></td><td>Self-efficacy; computing identity</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Academic development</b></td><td>Research skill proficiency</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Professional aspirations</b></td><td>Intentions to pursue graduate school; career goals</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="narrow" style="margin-top:1rem">
      The REU PI is responsible for disseminating the pre-test and post-test surveys to students. Students
      can opt in to provide contact information for the follow-up survey, which is sent to them directly.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
